# {{NAME}}

{{DESCRIPTION}}

Generated from xInsp2's **{{TEMPLATE_ID}}** plugin template (standalone mode). The
in-project equivalent is created by the VS Code command *xInsp2: New Project Plugin…*.

## Build

```sh
mkdir build && cd build
cmake ..
cmake --build . --config Release
```

The DLL lands at `build/{{NAME}}.dll`. Drop into a project's `plugins/{{NAME}}/` folder
or any backend's global plugins directory.

## Dependencies (extra DLLs)

If this plugin links a third-party DLL (beyond OpenCV — the backend already ships
OpenCV next to its exe), **put that DLL inside this plugin's folder**, next to
`{{NAME}}.dll`. The backend loads plugins with
`LoadLibraryEx(..., LOAD_LIBRARY_SEARCH_DLL_LOAD_DIR)`, so a plugin's own folder is
searched for its dependency DLLs. (The backend's app dir + System32 are still
searched; CWD and PATH are not.)

> ⚠️ **One process, one DLL per name.** Every plugin loads into the *same* backend
> process, and Windows keeps a single module per DLL **base name** per process. So
> two plugins that need *different versions of the same-named DLL* (e.g. both want
> `foo.dll`, one v1.2 and one v3.1) collide — whichever loads first wins and the
> other silently gets the wrong version. This bites dependencies resolved **by
> name** (i.e. a normal static import / linking `foo.lib`); a dependency you load
> yourself **by absolute path** keys on the full path and does *not* clash. To make
> two static deps coexist, give them **different file names** and link each plugin
> against its own name (renaming a prebuilt DLL isn't enough — the import name is
> baked in at link time), static-link, or pin one shared version. True same-name
> side-by-side versioning of a static import needs process isolation, which xInsp2
> doesn't provide (plugins run in-process for speed). Demo + proof:
> `examples/dll_version_clash`.

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `src/plugin.cpp` | Plugin class + `XI_PLUGIN_IMPL` |
| `plugin.json` | Manifest read by the backend's plugin scanner |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | Standalone build via the SDK's `xinsp2_add_plugin` macro |

## The template spine

All three templates are **one `xi::Plugin` skeleton** with progressively more
layers turned on — same base class, same helpers, never a different style:

| Tier | Layer | Adds |
|---|---|---|
| **easy** (this one) | 0 | `process()` only — the bare skeleton |
| **medium** | 1 | + config/params (`get_def`/`set_def`/`exchange` via `xi::Json`) + `status()` + a real image op |
| **expert** | 2 | + a background source worker (`xi::spawn_worker`) pushing frames via `emit()` |

Because every tier is `public xi::Plugin`, every tier gets `pool_image()`,
`status()`, `compress()`, `emit()`, and the capability wrappers for free. Pick a
tier by how much you need, not by a different architecture.

## API surface this template demonstrates

- `using xi::Plugin::Plugin` — inherit the ctor that wires `host_` / `name_`
- `process(Record)` — the per-frame hook (the one member this tier overrides)
- `pool_image()` + `xi::as_cv_read` / `xi::as_cv_write` — zero-copy image I/O

For the next step up, see the **medium** template (config + image processing) or
**expert** (background source thread).
